      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND/OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "While most \"fetal programming\" area focused on metabolic disease, intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) is also associated with a preference for less healthy food. Post-natal factors such as strained maternal-child interactions are equally related to obesogenic eating behaviors. We investigated if IUGR and the quality of the mother/child relationship affect emotional overeating in children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS/METHODS",
            "content": "Participants were 196 children from a prospective birth cohort (the MAVAN project). As part of the protocol at 4 years of age, mothers completed the Children Eating Behavior Questionnaire (CEBQ) and mother-child interactions were scored during a structured task. A GLM adjusted for BMI examined the interaction between the \"Atmosphere\" score (ATM) task, sex and IUGR on the emotional over-eating domain of the CEBQ."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "There was a significant interaction of BWR vs. sex vs. ATM (P\u2009=\u2009.02), with no effects of IUGR, sex or ATM. The model was significant for girls with low ATM scores (B\u2009=\u2009-2.035, P\u2009=\u2009.014), but not for girls with high (P\u2009=\u20090.94) or boys with high (P\u2009=\u2009.27) or low (P\u2009=\u2009.19) ATM scores. Only in IUGR girls, 48 months emotional over-eating correlated with BMI at that age (r\u2009=\u20090.560, P\u2009=\u20090.013) and predicted BMI in the subsequent years (r\u2009=\u20090.654, P\u2009=\u20090.006 at 60 months and r\u2009=\u20090.750, P\u2009=\u20090.005 at 72 months)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "IUGR and exposure to a negative emotional atmosphere during maternal-child interactions predicted emotional overeating in girls but not in boys. The quality of mother-infant interaction may be an important target for interventions to prevent emotional overeating and overweight in early development, particularly in girls with a history of IUGR."
          }
        ]
      },
